===Etymology 1===
See {{l|lv|siens}}.

====Noun====
{{head|lv|noun form|g=m}}

# {{lv-inflection of|siens|gen|s}}

===Etymology 2===
{{slim-wikipedia|lang=lv}}
[[File:Pared adobes.png|215px|thumb|Mājas siena (1)]]
[[File:Astorga muralla JMM.JPG|215px|thumb|Pilsētas siena (2)]]
[[File:St. Michael's Cave walls.jpg|215px|thumb|Alas sienas (4)]]
Cognate with {{cog|lt|síena||wall, border}}. Derived from verb {{m|lv|siet||to tie, bind}} in its older meaning “to braid, to weave” (see etymology of {{m|lv|siet}}), which suggests that the original meaning of {{m|lv|siena}} was “wicker-work”; compare cognates {{cog|ae|𐬵𐬌𐬥𐬎|t=ties, bonds, hobble, chain}}, {{cog|sga|sin||chain, necktie}}. The current meaning suggests that house walls were originally woven with branches and twigs, and then probably covered with mud (compare with {{cog|de|Wand||wall}}, {{m|de|winden||wind, twist, weave}}); some evidence from old folk songs supports this idea.<ref>{{R:lv:LEV}}</ref>

====Pronunciation====
* {{lv-IPA|siêna|broken}}
{{rfap|lv}}

